    {
      "id": "why",
      "type": "note.text",
      "position": {
        "x": 60,
        "y": -180
      },
      "params": {
        "text": "### What this graph answers\n\nWhat the cells look like. One search feeds two morphology queries — the skeletons and the\nsynapse locations — and the 3D viewer draws both in the same space.\n\nBoth queries are expensive, so nothing is fetched until you press Run. That is the hybrid\nmodel: cheap nodes re-run as you edit, network calls wait to be asked.",
        "outline": true
      },
      "size": {
        "width": 660,
        "height": 165
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "step1",
      "type": "note.text",
      "position": {
        "x": 476,
        "y": 620
      },
      "params": {
        "text": "**One search, two questions.** Both morphology nodes read the same neuron list, so the\nskeletons and the synapses are guaranteed to be about the same cells. **Warn above** on each\nis set low here, so a first look at a type says what it is about to pull rather than\nquietly pulling hundreds of megabytes.",
        "outline": true
      },
      "size": {
        "width": 520,
        "height": 135
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "step2",
      "type": "note.text",
      "position": {
        "x": 1308,
        "y": 620
      },
      "params": {
        "text": "**Colour comes from the table.** Skeletons and points each arrive with an attribute row per\nitem, so colouring by cell type or by synapse polarity is a column picker rather than a special\ncase inside the viewer.",
        "outline": true
      },
      "size": {
        "width": 480,
        "height": 130
      }
    }
